# Testing Standards

## Structure

- Mirror production folder structure in `test/`.
- Group tests by observable behavior, not by private implementation details.
- Keep test file names predictable and easy to map back to source files.

## Test Design

- Follow AAA: Arrange, Act, Assert.
- Keep tests deterministic and isolated.
- Use descriptive names: `should_<behavior>_when_<condition>`.
- Validate behavior, not implementation details.
- Keep assertions close to the behavior they validate.

## Test Levels

- Unit tests validate small pure rules, mappers, entities, helpers, and typed
  errors.
- Service or component tests validate collaboration between nearby parts such as
  service and repository boundaries.
- Integration tests validate real contracts with FFI, native code, assets, or
  environment-dependent resources.
- E2E tests validate live database or driver behavior under controlled external
  configuration.

## Mocks and Fakes

- Mock external boundaries only, such as DB, FFI, process, network, or platform
  dependencies.
- Prefer real domain entities and value objects in unit tests.
- Use fakes when behavior matters more than interaction verification.
- Do not mock pure logic without a concrete reason.

## Failure Paths

- Prioritize critical paths and error handling.
- Cover boundary conditions and null or empty inputs.
- Validate expected failure behavior, not just that "an error happened."
- Add regression tests for every confirmed bug.

## Environment-Dependent Tests

- Gate live or environment-dependent tests clearly.
- Skip environment-driven tests with explicit messages when prerequisites are
  missing.
- Keep helpers for env loading and test setup centralized.

## Review Checklist

- [ ] Tests mirror the relevant source structure.
- [ ] Each test covers one observable behavior contract.
- [ ] External dependencies are isolated correctly.
- [ ] Critical and error paths are covered.
- [ ] The simplest test level that proves the behavior was chosen.
- [ ] Environment-dependent tests fail or skip clearly.
